<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="V850:LE:32:default">
<!--
    GayHydra #509: the V850 compiler spec names the CALLT base pointer in its
    unaffected-register list.  The register is defined as CTBP
    (Helpers/Register.sinc) and the standalone decompiler looks registers up
    case-sensitively, so the former lowercase spelling "ctbp" made every V850
    architecture fail to initialise ("Unknown register name: ctbp") before any
    function could be loaded.  The Java side maps register names
    case-insensitively, which is why the GUI never noticed.

    The function is one CALLT followed by a return through lp:
        0x1000: 03 02      callt 0x3
        0x1002: 7f 00      jmp [lp]
    CALLT reads CTBP twice (the table slot and the target base), so the
    decompile only succeeds once the architecture initialises, and the register
    prints with the unaff_ prefix only because the cspec marks it unaffected.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1000">
03027f00
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000" name="tcallt"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>lo fu tcallt</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="V850 ctbp #1: the architecture initialises and CTBP resolves as an unaffected register" min="1" max="1">int4 unaff_CTBP;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="V850 ctbp #2: the callt dispatch reads the table through CTBP" min="1" max="1">\(\*\(code \*\)\(unaff_CTBP \+ \(uint4\)\*\(uint2 \*\)\(unaff_CTBP \+ 6\)\)\)\(\);</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
